		{
			"id": "xEJ6LMcT",
			"type": "text",
			"x": -331.26794887033986,
			"y": -1071.8633913846488,
			"width": 1425.7452306078762,
			"height": 482.22222222222194,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b04",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 95018769,
			"version": 537,
			"versionNonce": 1978047681,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1785843770073,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "HOW THE CEA INFORMATION\nARCHITECTURE & CONTEXT\nMANAGEMENT SYSTEM\nWORKS AND EVOLVES",
			"rawText": "HOW THE CEA INFORMATION ARCHITECTURE & CONTEXT MANAGEMENT SYSTEM WORKS AND EVOLVES",
			"fontSize": 96.44444444444439,
			"fontFamily": 6,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "HOW THE CEA INFORMATION ARCHITECTURE & CONTEXT MANAGEMENT SYSTEM WORKS AND EVOLVES",
			"autoResize": false,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false
		},
		{
			"id": "8YJT0pDV",
			"type": "text",
			"x": 589.424796998928,
			"y": -529.664511765148,
			"width": 517.2597404573182,
			"height": 2425,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
			"backgroundColor": "transparent",
			"fillStyle": "solid",
			"strokeWidth": 2,
			"strokeStyle": "solid",
			"roughness": 1,
			"opacity": 100,
			"groupIds": [],
			"frameId": null,
			"index": "b05",
			"roundness": null,
			"seed": 1325523295,
			"version": 448,
			"versionNonce": 419580559,
			"isDeleted": false,
			"boundElements": [],
			"updated": 1785843733896,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "I am building a judgment layer for a marketing system.\nYou have to get the judgment right before you build the\nexecution bots. It flows top-down: first principles create\ntactics, and tactics create solutions. There are different\nphases of creation, from prep to post. You stack micro-\nsystems to build macro-systems. As you move from micro\nto macro, the system stops relying on strict rules and\nrelies entirely on taste and judgment.\n\nThe goal is a hard-coded decision logic. If we define the\nIdeal Customer Profile, the system extracts the core idea.\nFrom there, it asks the right AI agents to figure out the\nexact positioning, angles, and distribution. I am not\nbuilding another generic content spinner. I am mapping\nthe literal thought process of elite CMOs and 8-figure\nagency founders step by step.\n\nThis isn't a tool to just pump out posts. It is a\npsychological weapon. You figure out how a person\nthinks, modify it, and change their behavior. You are\ncreating precise stimuli to influence them. That is the first\nprinciple. It is the cornerstone of everything we are doing\nhere.\n\nThe process moves in stages. Between stages, there are\nstrict feedback loops. You ask a question, verify the\nanswer hits the objective, and only then move forward.\nYou figure out market sophistication. You scrape UGC.\nYou learn exactly how the market speaks from real-world\ndata. All of that feeds into the system. The system then\nreads the current zeitgeist, picks the right platform, and\nwrites the copy. It tells you exactly how, when, and where\nto post.\n\nThe information architecture is strict. We don't pull data\nfrom random books. We find people who deeply\nunderstand marketing and psychology. We take hours of\ntheir content and turn it into massive transcripts. Those go\nstraight into the 1. collected raw inputs folder inside R&D.\nThat folder holds the foundational data we need to build\nthe system.\n\nThe collected raw inputs folder holds 2-hour podcasts and\n10-hour courses. It holds the raw, uncut nuance. It\ncaptures the pure instinct that you can never get from a\nchecklist or a template. That is our edge.\n\nNext, we extract the mental models and compress them.\nWe move those into the 2. sorted raw inputs folder. These\nare usually the official course PDFs or Google Docs.\nSometimes I write a synthesized document myself based\non my own notes. Everything here is a PDF or a\nMarkdown file, managed directly inside Obsidian.\n\nCollected inputs are raw 200-page dumps. Sorted inputs\nare cut down and organized by theme. We cap every\ndocument at 2,500 words. This forces them to be dense,\n5-page chunks. This tight chunking is exactly what you\nneed to feed AI agents or build a RAG system. That is\nwhy the sorted folder exists.\n\nNow the real work happens in the 3. standardized macro\nsystems folder. You take the sorted inputs and map them\nout visually. You draw the decision trees, the loops, and\nthe logic. You are taking raw information and structuring it\ninto a machine. This is Context Engineering. You collect,\nsort, set in order, and standardize. The output is a literal\nproduction pipeline designed to make a human take\naction.\n\nThe whole R&D folder syncs to Google Drive. This lets me\nfeed any part of the system straight into NotebookLM,\nClaude, or ChatGPT. If I create a Google Doc with my\nnotes, I just move it into the folder. A Python script\nwatches the folder, grabs the Doc, and instantly\ndownloads a clean PDF version.\n\nIf I drop a massive transcript into the sorted inputs folder,\nthe system automatically slices it into small chunks and\ntags the headings. This runs on a Python script. The script\njust runs in the background. As long as it is running, the\nfolder organizes itself.\n\nThis means anyone can download the R&D folder and\nconnect it to their own Obsidian vault. It is always live. A\nteam can drop raw inputs into the same folder. I can look\nat their inputs, sort them, and map them into the macro\nsystems. We end up with an execution-heavy\ninfrastructure built on high-level taste and judgment.\n\nWe diagram everything using Mermaid inside Excalidraw.\nBecause it sits inside Obsidian, it supports plain\nMarkdown. This means I can edit the visual diagrams\nthrough code, through AI, or by dragging boxes manually.\nI can hyperlink any box in the diagram straight to the exact\nPDF or Markdown file it references. You get a live,\nclickable visual map of the entire marketing brain.",
			"rawText": "I am building a judgment layer for a marketing system. You have to get the judgment right before you build the execution bots. It flows top-down: first principles create tactics, and tactics create solutions. There are different phases of creation, from prep to post. You stack micro-systems to build macro-systems. As you move from micro to macro, the system stops relying on strict rules and relies entirely on taste and judgment.\n\nThe goal is a hard-coded decision logic. If we define the Ideal Customer Profile, the system extracts the core idea. From there, it asks the right AI agents to figure out the exact positioning, angles, and distribution. I am not building another generic content spinner. I am mapping the literal thought process of elite CMOs and 8-figure agency founders step by step.\n\nThis isn't a tool to just pump out posts. It is a psychological weapon. You figure out how a person thinks, modify it, and change their behavior. You are creating precise stimuli to influence them. That is the first principle. It is the cornerstone of everything we are doing here.\n\nThe process moves in stages. Between stages, there are strict feedback loops. You ask a question, verify the answer hits the objective, and only then move forward. You figure out market sophistication. You scrape UGC. You learn exactly how the market speaks from real-world data. All of that feeds into the system. The system then reads the current zeitgeist, picks the right platform, and writes the copy. It tells you exactly how, when, and where to post.\n\nThe information architecture is strict. We don't pull data from random books. We find people who deeply understand marketing and psychology. We take hours of their content and turn it into massive transcripts. Those go straight into the 1. collected raw inputs folder inside R&D. That folder holds the foundational data we need to build the system.\n\nThe collected raw inputs folder holds 2-hour podcasts and 10-hour courses. It holds the raw, uncut nuance. It captures the pure instinct that you can never get from a checklist or a template. That is our edge.\n\nNext, we extract the mental models and compress them. We move those into the 2. sorted raw inputs folder. These are usually the official course PDFs or Google Docs. Sometimes I write a synthesized document myself based on my own notes. Everything here is a PDF or a Markdown file, managed directly inside Obsidian.\n\nCollected inputs are raw 200-page dumps. Sorted inputs are cut down and organized by theme. We cap every document at 2,500 words. This forces them to be dense, 5-page chunks. This tight chunking is exactly what you need to feed AI agents or build a RAG system. That is why the sorted folder exists.\n\nNow the real work happens in the 3. standardized macro systems folder. You take the sorted inputs and map them out visually. You draw the decision trees, the loops, and the logic. You are taking raw information and structuring it into a machine. This is Context Engineering. You collect, sort, set in order, and standardize. The output is a literal production pipeline designed to make a human take action.\n\nThe whole R&D folder syncs to Google Drive. This lets me feed any part of the system straight into NotebookLM, Claude, or ChatGPT. If I create a Google Doc with my notes, I just move it into the folder. A Python script watches the folder, grabs the Doc, and instantly downloads a clean PDF version.\n\nIf I drop a massive transcript into the sorted inputs folder, the system automatically slices it into small chunks and tags the headings. This runs on a Python script. The script just runs in the background. As long as it is running, the folder organizes itself.\n\nThis means anyone can download the R&D folder and connect it to their own Obsidian vault. It is always live. A team can drop raw inputs into the same folder. I can look at their inputs, sort them, and map them into the macro systems. We end up with an execution-heavy infrastructure built on high-level taste and judgment.\n\nWe diagram everything using Mermaid inside Excalidraw. Because it sits inside Obsidian, it supports plain Markdown. This means I can edit the visual diagrams through code, through AI, or by dragging boxes manually. I can hyperlink any box in the diagram straight to the exact PDF or Markdown file it references. You get a live, clickable visual map of the entire marketing brain.",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 6,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "top",
			"containerId": null,
			"originalText": "I am building a judgment layer for a marketing system. You have to get the judgment right before you build the execution bots. It flows top-down: first principles create tactics, and tactics create solutions. There are different phases of creation, from prep to post. You stack micro-systems to build macro-systems. As you move from micro to macro, the system stops relying on strict rules and relies entirely on taste and judgment.\n\nThe goal is a hard-coded decision logic. If we define the Ideal Customer Profile, the system extracts the core idea. From there, it asks the right AI agents to figure out the exact positioning, angles, and distribution. I am not building another generic content spinner. I am mapping the literal thought process of elite CMOs and 8-figure agency founders step by step.\n\nThis isn't a tool to just pump out posts. It is a psychological weapon. You figure out how a person thinks, modify it, and change their behavior. You are creating precise stimuli to influence them. That is the first principle. It is the cornerstone of everything we are doing here.\n\nThe process moves in stages. Between stages, there are strict feedback loops. You ask a question, verify the answer hits the objective, and only then move forward. You figure out market sophistication. You scrape UGC. You learn exactly how the market speaks from real-world data. All of that feeds into the system. The system then reads the current zeitgeist, picks the right platform, and writes the copy. It tells you exactly how, when, and where to post.\n\nThe information architecture is strict. We don't pull data from random books. We find people who deeply understand marketing and psychology. We take hours of their content and turn it into massive transcripts. Those go straight into the 1. collected raw inputs folder inside R&D. That folder holds the foundational data we need to build the system.\n\nThe collected raw inputs folder holds 2-hour podcasts and 10-hour courses. It holds the raw, uncut nuance. It captures the pure instinct that you can never get from a checklist or a template. That is our edge.\n\nNext, we extract the mental models and compress them. We move those into the 2. sorted raw inputs folder. These are usually the official course PDFs or Google Docs. Sometimes I write a synthesized document myself based on my own notes. Everything here is a PDF or a Markdown file, managed directly inside Obsidian.\n\nCollected inputs are raw 200-page dumps. Sorted inputs are cut down and organized by theme. We cap every document at 2,500 words. This forces them to be dense, 5-page chunks. This tight chunking is exactly what you need to feed AI agents or build a RAG system. That is why the sorted folder exists.\n\nNow the real work happens in the 3. standardized macro systems folder. You take the sorted inputs and map them out visually. You draw the decision trees, the loops, and the logic. You are taking raw information and structuring it into a machine. This is Context Engineering. You collect, sort, set in order, and standardize. The output is a literal production pipeline designed to make a human take action.\n\nThe whole R&D folder syncs to Google Drive. This lets me feed any part of the system straight into NotebookLM, Claude, or ChatGPT. If I create a Google Doc with my notes, I just move it into the folder. A Python script watches the folder, grabs the Doc, and instantly downloads a clean PDF version.\n\nIf I drop a massive transcript into the sorted inputs folder, the system automatically slices it into small chunks and tags the headings. This runs on a Python script. The script just runs in the background. As long as it is running, the folder organizes itself.\n\nThis means anyone can download the R&D folder and connect it to their own Obsidian vault. It is always live. A team can drop raw inputs into the same folder. I can look at their inputs, sort them, and map them into the macro systems. We end up with an execution-heavy infrastructure built on high-level taste and judgment.\n\nWe diagram everything using Mermaid inside Excalidraw. Because it sits inside Obsidian, it supports plain Markdown. This means I can edit the visual diagrams through code, through AI, or by dragging boxes manually. I can hyperlink any box in the diagram straight to the exact PDF or Markdown file it references. You get a live, clickable visual map of the entire marketing brain.",
